PRECEDENCE: for existing or resumed code, preserve the code and history, and prove it with tests that fail without it instead of deleting it.

READ:
(1) Change card description at docs/plan/E07/E07-F01/E07-F01.md
(2) Local standards, each one that exists:
    - `CLAUDE.md` and `AGENTS.md`
    - `docs/architecture/coding-standards.md` and the standards sub-files it links, including the Quality Limits and Testing Standards sections
    - `docs/architecture/patterns-catalog.md`
    - the Quality Gate section of `docs/architecture/tech-stack.md`
(3) Existing code in the affected areas

IMPLEMENT the change:

Step 1 — VERIFY AND IMPLEMENT:
TDD PROCESS:
Choose verification by artifact type. This table decides which verification applies and overrides any red-first wording in the included skills; those skills supply the red-green procedure for behavior-changing code. A change that mixes types applies each row to its own part.

| Change type | Verification |
|---|---|
| Behavior-changing code | Red first: the failing test is observed before implementation |
| Refactor with no behavior change | Existing tests are green before and after; no new red step is required |
| Prompt, documentation or configuration | The test plan's planned check for the TC (render, include resolution, validator); a source-text search only when the literal is an explicit contract |

When the work item has a test plan, read the feature test plan and then:
- Write one test per TC, with variants as separate cases. Each test name contains the TC-ID.
- Assert the TC's **Expected Output** exactly.
- Keep the TC's Caller-Path Contract rules: drive the declared entrypoint with the production argument shape, mock no higher than the lowest mock seam, never touch a forbidden mock, and satisfy the counter-factual (the check fails against an empty or buggy implementation).
- Never edit a TC's Preconditions, Input or Expected Output; return `blocked` with the specific conflict instead.

Do not write a test that searches source text unless the searched literal is an explicit contract.

Follow the project's documented test architecture and mocking boundaries, and run the focused test with the project's test command:
1. For behavior-changing code, confirm the test fails for the right reason (the missing behavior, not an import error or fixture bug).
2. Implement the minimum code to pass (green). Do NOT add features beyond what the work item requires.
3. Refactor while keeping tests green.

Tests that mock above the declared entrypoint, or use argument shapes production never passes, will be rejected at code review.
Follow existing patterns in the codebase and the local standards.
Resolve each limit as the Craft Rubric below directs, and write within the resolved limits on the first pass. When the project declares a measuring command, run it on the changed files before exit.

Step 2 — QUALITY GATE (MANDATORY):
Run the resolved quality gate commands before advancing. Fix ALL failures before advancing. No exceptions.
A failure that predates your change and sits outside the files you changed: fix it when the fix is straightforward and within scope; otherwise route it as a bug through the triage skill. Either way, while the gate fails it is unmet: recommend `fail`.

Step 3 — SELF-REVIEW (before exit):
Run the self-review checklist in the Craft Rubric below against your own diff. Fix every finding, or record a cited reason why the item is not applicable. Re-run the quality gate after any fix.

EXIT GATE:
- Change implemented per description
- Tests pass
- Quality gate passes
- Evidence walk: `gate_result.summary` names the test or check for each AC ID
- Every new or changed function and file is within the resolved limits, with each limit's source label stated
- Self-review is recorded with no unresolved finding

## Reference Methodology

# Craft Rubric

Development and review grade code against this one rubric: authors run it on
their own diff before exit, and reviewers apply the same limits and severity.

## Resolving the limits

Look for the `## Quality Limits` table in the project's local
`coding-standards.md` or a standards sub-file it links. Resolve each limit
independently. A declared limit covers the code its `applies to` column names.

| Local Quality Limits row for this limit | Value used | Source label | When new or substantially expanded code exceeds it |
|---|---|---|---|
| Present with a value | Local value | "declared in local standards (§Quality Limits)" | Blocker |
| Present but marked "proposed — confirm" | Local value | "proposed in local standards, unconfirmed" | Same as a generic default |
| Absent, section missing, or value blank or a placeholder | Generic default below | "generic default — not declared in local standards" | Admitted only with evidenced impact |

## Generic defaults

Use a default only when the local row is absent, as described above.

| Limit | Generic default |
|---|---|
| Cyclomatic complexity | 10 |
| Function length (lines) | 50 |
| File length (lines) | 500 |
| Nesting depth | 4 |

Each limit is an inclusive maximum: a measurement equal to the limit passes,
and one more is over it. A coverage target is optional and has no generic
default; with no local row, no percentage gates the change. For coverage,
"over the limit" means falling short of the target.

## Source label

State the source label on every finding or self-review line that cites a limit.
A line that relies on a generic default carries the label
"generic default — not declared in local standards" and recommends declaring the
limit through a standards refresh (bootstrap fill-gaps). A missing limit never
blocks work by being missing.

## Severity

- **Locally declared limit.** New or substantially expanded code over it is a
  blocker, because it breaks a documented standard. A declared limit is a
  documented standard, not a scoring threshold.
- **Generic default or unconfirmed proposal.** The number is a signal. Admit a
  finding only with evidenced impact under the admission criteria in
  `skills/quality/context/review-rubric.md`, which also set its severity.
- **Already over a limit.** Code that was over a limit before the change, and
  that the change does not substantially expand, is a non-blocker routed to
  triage, whatever the limit's source.

## Self-review checklist

Apply every item to the diff. An author fixes each finding, or records a cited
reason why the item is not applicable.

### Reuse

- Search the codebase for an existing implementation before adding a utility,
  helper or abstraction, and call the existing one when it fits.
- Record the search, including a search that found no duplicate.

### Standards citation

- Check the changed code against the project's local standards documents.
- Cite each violation as
  `{file:line} — {rule} — {standards document §section "quoted clause"}`.
- When no clause covers a concern, report the gap and do not call it a standards
  violation. Keep it only with evidence of a concrete user, behavior, invariant,
  security or maintenance consequence.
- A demonstrated failure of a documented input or acceptance criterion is a
  correctness finding even without a clause.

### SOLID and architecture

- Single responsibility: each class or module has one clear job.
- Open/closed: stable code paths extend without modification.
- Apply Liskov substitution, interface segregation and dependency inversion
  where relevant.
- Respect the layering, dependency direction and integration points in the
  project's architecture documents.

### Idiom

- Follow the community-standard patterns of the code's language and the
  project's own conventions; replace non-idiomatic constructs.
- Leave no commented-out code, debug output or premature abstraction.

### Complexity and size

- Check each new or changed function and file against the resolved limits for
  cyclomatic complexity, function length, file length and nesting depth.
- Use the project's declared measuring command or other tooling where available.
- Apply the severity rules above and state each limit's source label.

### Test design

- Each acceptance criterion has at least one test case.
- Boundaries cover minimum, maximum, empty, null, zero and negative one for each
  input.
- Negative cases assert what must not happen.
- Flaky risk: no dependence on timing, random data, network, real time or
  shared state.
- Mock discipline: a test that only asserts a mock was called does not test
  real behavior.
- Name any boundary or negative case that the criterion implies and the tests
  omit.


SUMMARY LINE: Begin `gate_result.summary` with the line `ACs verified: <ids>; red-first: <n>/<m>; commit: <sha>`. Write `none` when there are no AC IDs, `0/0` when no TC changes behavior, and `none` when there is no commit.

DECISION:
- Unclear requirements -> recommended_outcome: blocked; put the specific
  question in `gate_result.no_kickback_reason` rather than guessing
- Exit gate met -> recommended_outcome: pass
- Cannot implement (external blocker) -> recommended_outcome: blocked; state
  the blocker in `gate_result.no_kickback_reason`
- Otherwise unresolved -> recommended_outcome: fail. This outcome's role is
  `route_rework` — `gate_result.kickbacks` must stay empty; state the
  specific blockers in `gate_result.summary`.

FINAL RESPONSE — STRUCTURED GATE RESULT (T-E34-F05, `result_contract: gate_result_v1`):
Do NOT end your response with a `RECOMMENDED OUTCOME: <key>` line, a `PARENT NOTE: <text>` line, or a bare `<entity-id> -> <status> --reason "<why>"` kickback line — those are the legacy free-form directive grammar and this step does not use it. Instead, the ENTIRE trimmed final response must be exactly one worker-control envelope (`kind: final`), with a nested `gate_result` payload:

```
{
  "kind": "final",
  "recommended_outcome": "<the outcome key you are recommending, e.g. pass or fail>",
  "evidence": [],
  "gate_result": {
    "schema_version": 1,
    "summary": "<one paragraph: verdict and why>",
    "findings": [ ... ],
    "kickbacks": [ ... ],
    "no_kickback_reason": "<only when required — see below>"
  }
}
```

The empty arrays above are illustrative. If this prompt requires outer
`evidence` entries, include them; each is an object with `kind`, `pointer` and
`summary`.

`gate_result` fields (unknown fields are rejected):

| Object | Field | Rule |
|---|---|---|
| result | `schema_version` | `1` |
| result | `summary` | required, 1000 B max |
| result | `no_kickback_reason` | 1000 B max |
| `findings[]` | `severity` | required, 256 B max |
| `findings[]` | `class_key` | required, 256 B max |
| `findings[]` | `class_statement` | required, 1000 B max |
| `findings[]` | `fingerprint` | required, 256 B max |
| `findings[]` | `disposition` | required: `open`, `fixed`, `already_dispositioned`, `severity_conflict` or `not_reproducible` |
| `findings[]` | `disposition_pointer` | 2048 B max; required for `already_dispositioned` and `severity_conflict` |
| `findings[]` | `affected_ids` | optional strings, 256 B max each |
| `findings[]` | `evidence_pointers` | optional strings, 2048 B max each |
| `kickbacks[]` | `entity_key` | required, 256 B max |
| `kickbacks[]` | `target_status` | required, 256 B max |
| `kickbacks[]` | `reason` | required, 1000 B max |

Every array holds at most 100 items and the whole `gate_result` at most
256 KiB. Omit `remediation_sweeps` and `change_impacts` unless this prompt or
a skill it names defines their contents for this step. A `remediation_sweeps[]`
entry that is `open` with `open_count: 0` (no feasible guard) must point
`guard.counterfactual_pointer` at a project-local `{"kind": "open_sweep_link",
"class_key", "work_item_key", "reason"}` record; `work_item_key` must resolve
to an existing task, feature, epic, bug, change-card, or tech-debt entity
naming the class (note keys do not resolve).

Rules this step's outcomes commonly need:

For each `kickbacks[]` entry, `target_status` is required by GateResult v1
but is only a compatibility assertion. Read the target entity's current
status and its workflow step, then set `target_status` to that step's
`rework_outcomes[recommended_outcome]`. Do not choose or infer a destination
independently. If the route is missing or cannot be read, do not guess or
submit that kickback; report the missing route and return a blocked result.

- Whether `kickbacks` must be empty or non-empty for a given outcome depends
  on that outcome's configured semantic role (`outcome_roles`, visible via
  `shark get E07-F01 --json` or `shark next E07-F01 --json` ->
  `result_contract`/`outcome_roles`), not on this prompt's own text:
  - role `success` (typically `pass`): zero `kickbacks`, and no `findings`
    left with `disposition: open` or `severity_conflict`.
  - role `kickback_rework` (a per-item rework outcome): at least one
    `kickbacks` entry (`entity_key`, `target_status`, `reason`) — one per
    item that must reopen, replacing the legacy
    `<entity-id> -> <status> --reason "<why>"` line format.
  - role `route_rework` (this step's own main entity re-routes as a whole):
    zero `kickbacks` — the configured route itself is the rework path; do
    not also emit per-item kickbacks for this role.
  - roles `blocked`/`hold`/`cancelled`: either at least one kickback or a
    non-empty `no_kickback_reason` explaining why none applies.
- A blocking finding you are reporting (not fixing) must carry
  `disposition: open` (or `severity_conflict` when reviewers disagree);
  every other GateResult field constraint is enforced the same way
  regardless of which role this outcome carries.

If this step names or requires a report file, write the full narrative there
and keep `summary` a compact verdict statement. Otherwise the structured
findings and `summary` are the record; do not invent a report location.
